 of non-background objects:\n- **Paths:** bent lines, one cell wide, all in one colour (for example the 3-coloured L shape in pair 1).\n- **Markers:** single cells, each in its own colour.\n\n**How each path finds its marker.** Each path has exactly one marker lined up with one of its two ends. If you keep going straight past that end, in the direction of the path's last step, you reach the marker after crossing some background cells. Call the number of those empty cells *n*.\n\n**What changes: the path moves forward like a snake.**\n- The path grows *n* cells from its head, in its own colour, until it touches the marker.\n- The *n* cells at the other end (the tail) are repainted in the marker's colour, as if the snake left a trail behind it.\n- The amount of the original colour therefore stays the same. Only the tail cells take the marker colour.\n\n**Checking this against the fact sheet:**\n- **Pair 1, path 3:**\n  - Head at (6,6), pointing down. Marker 8 sits at (10,6), so 3 cells (7..9,6) are added in colour 3.\n  - The tail (4,2..4) becomes 8, which is also 3 cells.\n- **Pair 1, path 6:**\n  - Head at (10,10), pointing up. Marker 4 sits at (5,10), so 4 cells (6..9,10) are added.\n  - The tail (15,4..7) becomes 4.\n- **Pair 2, path 1:**\n  - Head at (22,9), pointing left. Marker 6 sits at (22,3), so 5 cells are added.\n  - The tail is recoloured *along the path order*: (25,13), (25,12), (25,11), (24,11), (23,11). It goes around the corner. So the tail must be measured along the path, not inside a bounding box.\n- **Pair 3** works the same way (extensions of 3 and 6).\n\n**Test input:**\n- **Path 2:** its end (5,6) points right toward marker 6 at (5,10).\n  - 3 cells (5,7..9) are added in colour 2.\n  - The tail (2,2), (2,3), (3,3) turns 6.\n- **Path 3:** its end (13,12) points up toward marker 4 at (8,12).\n  - 4 cells (9..12,12) are added in colour 3.\n  - The 4 tail cells starting at (11,5), going down to (14,5), turn 4.\n\n**Construction stages.** For each path, the program emits two canvases: one after drawing the extension, and one after repainting the tail. It then emits the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    singles = {o[\"cells\"][0]: o[\"colour\"] for o in objs if o[\"size\"] == 1}\n    for o in objs:\n        if o[\"size\"] < 2:\n            continue\n        cells = set(o[\"cells\"])\n        def nbrs(c):\n            y, x = c\n            return [(y+dy, x+dx) for dy, dx in ((1,0),(-1,0),(0,1),(0,-1)) if (y+dy, x+dx) in cells]\n        ends = [c for c in cells if len(nbrs(c)) == 1]\n        if len(ends) != 2:\n            continue\n        # order path from ends[0]\n        path = [ends[0]]; prev = None\n        while True:\n            nx = [c for c in nbrs(path[-1]) if c != prev]\n            if not nx:\n                break\n            prev = path[-1]; path.append(nx[0])\n        done = False\n        for head_idx in (0, -1):\n            path_o = path if head_idx == -1 else path[::-1]  # head at end of list\n            head, before = path_o[-1], path_o[-2]\n            dy, dx = head[0]-before[0], head[1]-before[1]\n            y, x = head[0]+dy, head[1]+dx\n            ext = []\n            while 0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W and g[y, x] == bg:\n                ext.append((y, x)); y += dy; x += dx\n            if 0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W and (y, x) in singles and ext:\n                mcol = singles[(y, x)]\n                for c in ext:\n                    out[c] = o[\"colour\"]\n                emit(out, f\"Extend colour-{o['colour']} path by {len(ext)} cells from its head until it touches the colour-{mcol} marker.\")\n                for c in path_o[:len(ext)]:\n                    out[c] = mcol\n                emit(out, f\"Repaint the last {len(ext)} tail cells of that path with the marker colour {mcol}.\")\n                done = True\n                break\n    emit(out, \"Final grid: every path has slid forward to its marker, leaving a marker-coloured trail.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"recolour":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The tail is recoloured *along the path order*: (25,13), (25,12), (25,11), (24,11), (23,11)."}],"paths":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Paths:** bent lines, one cell wide, all in one colour (for example the 3-coloured L shape in pair 1)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**How each path finds its marker.** Each path has exactly one marker lined up with one of its two ends."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"If you keep going straight past that end, in the direction of the path's last step, you reach the marker after crossing some background cells."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Call the number of those empty cells *n*."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"86d2c1a31fb76c884fcacfa48fd09f78e2aac5161f3ed8cef77da1d77c5f996b","task_sha256":"beec20f41fd0d317ebcecb8031d09bcd638070bf6e45f72c65564b1e948046fe","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
